   15
  ],
  "printed": "Isaiah xxxviii. 15",
  "page": 409,
  "line": 23,
  "situation": "The second twin of §37, and it differs from its Part II fellow twice over. The chapter figure reads Is. xxxiii. 15 — *he that walketh righteously*, which is the opposite of what the line wants. The verse is Hezekiah's, Isaiah xxxviii. 15, and printed 372–373 cites it rightly. And the sentence itself has changed. Both recensions turn the Vulgate's *cum ipse fecerit* — when He hath done it — into *fecerim*, when I have; but §37 stammers it, *fecerim, fecerim, fecerim*, three times and with no object, where here it is said once and given one: *cum ipse hæc fecerim*. Where Part II could not get past the word, Part III names the thing done. Neither recension is conformed to the other, and the triple stands as a triple there.",
  "source": "identified",
  "band": "R",
  "numbering": true
 },
